## 3. Schema Design

All tables are defined in a single `schema.yaml` file.  
This makes it easy to add or modify fields without touching code.

```yaml
# schema.yaml
database: synthetic_health.db

tables:
  patients:
    fields:
      patient_id:              { type: int,      generator: uuid4 }
      first_name:              { type: str,      generator: name_first }
      last_name:               { type: str,      generator: name_last }
      gender:                  { type: str,      generator: gender }
      birth_date:              { type: date,     generator: birth_date }
      race:                    { type: str,      generator: race }
      ethnicity:               { type: str,      generator: ethnicity }
      address:                 { type: str,      generator: address }
      phone:                   { type: str,      generator: phone_number }
      email:                   { type: str,      generator: email }
      insurance_id:            { type: str,      generator: insurance_id }

  visits:
    fields:
      visit_id:                { type: int,      generator: uuid4 }
      patient_id:              { type: int,      generator: patient_ref }
      visit_date:              { type: date,     generator: visit_date }
      provider:                { type: str,      generator: provider_name }
      reason:                  { type: str,      generator: reason }

  diagnoses:
    fields:
      diagnosis_id:           { type: int,      generator: uuid4 }
      visit_id:               { type: int,      generator: visit_ref }
      code:                   { type: str,      generator: icd10 }
      description:            { type: str,      generator: icd10_desc }

  medications:
    fields:
      medication_id:          { type: int,      generator: uuid4 }
      visit_id:               { type: int,      generator: visit_ref }
      name:                   { type: str,      generator: drug_name }
      dosage:                 { type: str,      generator: dosage }
      frequency:              { type: str,      generator: frequency }

  labs:
    fields:
      lab_id:                 { type: int,      generator: uuid4 }
      visit_id:               { type: int,      generator: visit_ref }
      test_name:              { type: str,      generator: lab_test }
      result:                 { type: float,    generator: lab_result }
      unit:                   { type: str,      generator: unit }
      reference_range:        { type: str,      generator: reference_range }

  vitals:
    fields:
      vital_id:               { type: int,      generator: uuid4 }
      visit_id:               { type: int,      generator: visit_ref }
      temperature:            { type: float,    generator: temperature }
      blood_pressure:         { type: str,      generator: blood_pressure }
      heart_rate:             { type: int,      generator: heart_rate }
      respiratory_rate:       { type: int,      generator: respiratory_rate }
```

> **Note** – Each field’s `generator` key maps to a function that will return a random value.  
> The `patient_ref` and `visit_ref` generators are special: they pick an existing ID from the referenced table to maintain referential integrity.

